
.TH CKSUM 1 "October 2026" "cksum" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cksum \- calculate CRC checksums and file lengths
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cksum
.RB [ \-HIPLN ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.PP
.B crc32
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cksum
calculates a cyclic redundancy check (CRC) checksum for each
specified file and prints the checksum, file length in bytes,
and filename.
.PP
If no files are specified, standard input is read.
A filename of
.B \-
also refers to standard input.
.PP
By default,
.B cksum
uses the POSIX CRC algorithm with a big-endian CRC calculation,
includes the file length in the CRC calculation, and applies
a final bitwise inversion.
.PP
When invoked as
.BR crc32 ,
the program calculates a standard reflected CRC-32 checksum,
printing eight hexadecimal digits without the file length.
This is equivalent to invoking
.B cksum
with the options
.BR \-HPLN .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-H
Print the checksum as eight hexadecimal digits instead of decimal.
.TP
.B \-I
Skip the final bitwise inversion of the CRC value.
.TP
.B \-P
Initialize the CRC calculation with all bits set to one
instead of zero.
.TP
.B \-L
Use the reflected, little-endian CRC algorithm instead of
the default big-endian algorithm.
.TP
.B \-N
Do not include the file length in the CRC calculation,
and omit the length from the output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
The default output format is:
.PP
.RS
.I checksum length filename
.RE
.PP
The length is expressed in bytes.
When reading from standard input without a file argument,
the filename is omitted.
.PP
With
.BR \-N ,
the length field is omitted.
With
.BR \-H ,
the checksum is printed in hexadecimal.
.PP
When invoked as
.BR crc32 ,
the output consists of an eight-digit hexadecimal checksum,
followed by the filename when one was specified.
.SH EXAMPLES
Calculate the POSIX checksum of a file:
.PP
.RS
.B cksum file.txt
.RE
.PP
Print the checksum in hexadecimal:
.PP
.RS
.B cksum -H file.txt
.RE
.PP
Calculate a standard CRC-32 checksum:
.PP
.RS
.B crc32 file.txt
.RE
.PP
Calculate CRC-32 using the
.B cksum
command:
.PP
.RS
.B cksum -HPLN file.txt
.RE
.PP
Calculate checksums for multiple files:
.PP
.RS
.B cksum file1.txt file2.txt
.RE
.PP
Calculate a checksum from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.B echo hello | cksum
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option was specified or an error occurred
while opening or reading a file.
.SH STANDARDS
The default checksum calculation follows the POSIX
.B cksum
algorithm.
The additional options and the
.B crc32
invocation are extensions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sha256sum (1),
.BR md5sum (1)

